/// The consumer declares its own `String` host type and imports the
/// dependency's `Box` newtype, whose constructor/projector PAYLOAD is the
/// dependency's *own* `String`. Identity-exact nominal typing
/// (`specs/language.md` § Nominal types) keeps the two `String`s distinct:
/// `String` here is `(main, String)`, the dependency's payload is
/// `(bdep/boxed, String)`. A cross-module newtype's member payload is
/// qualified in the newtype's *declaring* module, so the
/// dependency's `Box.mk` wants `(bdep/boxed, String)`. Feeding the
/// consumer's own `(main, String)` is therefore a type error in the
/// constructor's PAYLOAD slot — exit 14. Before the fix the typer
/// requalified `Box`'s payload in the *caller*, conflating the two
/// same-named `String`s by leaf name and wrongly accepting this.
module main;

import bdep/boxed(Box);

host type String role(str);

/// Build the dependency's `Box` from the consumer's own `String`. The
/// member payload distinctness rejects this: `Box.mk` wants the
/// dependency's `String`, not the consumer's.
pub fn mix(s: String) -> Box { Box.mk(s) }
